# Landing pages and conversion (LP)

This is the short form. Before writing code in this area, read the full rules with reasons, bad/good examples and checks: `.ui-polish/rules/25-landing-pages.md`.

- **LP-01 The hero says what, for whom, and what to do** (error). Without scrolling, at 375px and 1440px, the first screen shows: a headline that names what the product does in plain words (not a slogan, SL-07); one supporting sentence saying who it is for and the main outcome; one primary call to action that names the action ("Start free trial", "Book a demo") plus at most one secondary; and a visual of the actual product (LP-04). Keep the headline under about ten words and the supporting line under about 25.
- **LP-02 One goal per page, asked for at every decision point** (warning). Each landing page has one conversion goal (sign up, book a demo, join the waitlist) and every primary button on it serves that goal, with the same label. Repeat the call to action where a visitor is likely to decide: after the hero, after the proof section, after pricing, and at the end. Remove or demote competing exits (newsletter signup, social links, unrelated navigation) on dedicated campaign pages. On phones, keep the primary action reachable: a sticky bottom bar or a header button once the hero has scrolled away.
- **LP-03 Proof sits next to the claim** (warning). Back each major claim with evidence placed right beside it: customer logos (real, with permission, AS-04), testimonials with full name, role, company and photo, specific numbers with their source and date ("4,200 studios", "paid 11 days sooner on average, 2025 customer data"), ratings from named review sites, security and compliance badges you actually hold. Never invent any of it (CP-02, SL-11): if the owner has no proof yet, use the founder's story or a transparent beta note instead.
- **LP-04 Show the real product** (warning). Use real product screenshots or short recordings, cropped to the part that proves the point of the section, at native resolution, in a frame that matches the site's theme, with alt text describing what they show. Prefer one large, specific image per feature over icon grids (SL-08). Abstract illustrations, 3D blobs and stock photos of people at laptops do not count as showing the product (SL-12, AS-05). Keep screenshots current when the UI changes.
- **LP-05 Pricing is easy to choose from** (warning). Show two to four plans, with one marked as recommended (UX-05); name plans by who they are for; show the price with currency and period ("$12 per user / month, billed yearly"); if there is a monthly/yearly toggle, show which is selected and the saving in money, not only percent; list the three to five differences that matter first, with a full comparison table below; state what happens after a trial and whether a card is needed; and give the enterprise or custom option a clear next step rather than a bare "Contact us".
- **LP-06 Answer the objections before they leave** (info). Near the final call to action, answer the questions that stop people: an FAQ of five to eight real questions (price changes, data export, security, cancelling, support hours, who it is not for), visible contact options (email or chat, response time), and the guarantee or refund policy if one exists. Write answers in two or three sentences; link out for detail. Use a disclosure pattern (`<details>`) so the list stays short.
- **LP-07 Legal and contact links are present** (warning). Every marketing page links, usually from the footer, to a privacy policy, terms of service, and a way to contact the company (email or contact page); add a cookie notice only if the site sets non-essential cookies, and an imprint where the law requires one. Forms that collect personal data link to the privacy policy next to the submit button. Ask the owner for the legal text (AS-01); never generate legal text and present it as final.
- **LP-08 Lead forms ask for as little as possible** (warning). A signup, waitlist or demo form on a landing page asks only what is needed for the next step: email alone for a waitlist or trial, email plus name and company size for a demo. Offer single sign-on ("Continue with Google") where the product supports it. Put the form's promise next to the button ("No card needed", "We'll reply within a day"). Ask everything else later, inside the product (FM-06, UX-11). Confirm submission on the page with what happens next (UX-12).
